  "worked_solution": "We note that $p=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $p$ and $6p.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(p,6p)=6p.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $6p,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{p+6}{6p}-\\frac{2}{p} & =-\\frac{p-2}{6p} \\\\ 6p\\cdot (\\frac{p+6}{6p}-\\frac{2}{p}) & =6p\\cdot (-\\frac{p-2}{6p}) \\\\ 6p\\cdot \\frac{p+6}{6p}-6\\cdot p\\cdot \\frac{2}{p} & =6p(-\\frac{p-2}{6p}) \\\\ \\cancel{6p}\\cdot \\frac{p+6}{\\cancel{6p}}-6\\cdot \\cancel{p}\\frac{2}{\\cancel{p}} & =\\cancel{6p}(-\\frac{p-2}{\\cancel{6p}}) \\\\ p+6-12 & =-(p-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $p,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p+6-12 & =-(p-2) \\\\ p-6 & =-p+2 \\\\ 2p & =8 \\\\ p & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $p=0$ as a solution. However, since $p=4\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $p=4.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
